1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,486
Do you need shin guards and knee pads for short track speed skating?

2
00:00:04,486 --> 00:00:11,942
Yes, in some form, and you will learn which form, how to fit it, and how to find the gap most skaters leave open.

3
00:00:11,942 --> 00:00:16,890
Most national federations require cut resistant protection for competition.

4
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:22,696
Whether you must also wear separate hard pads depends on your federation and your event.

5
00:00:22,696 --> 00:00:26,259
So that part of the answer lives in your own rulebook.

6
00:00:26,259 --> 00:00:28,766
What almost nobody checks is the join.

7
00:00:28,766 --> 00:00:31,339
Plenty of club skaters own decent pads.

8
00:00:31,339 --> 00:00:38,003
They still step on with a strip of bare lycra between the boot cuff and the bottom of the shin guard.

9
00:00:38,003 --> 00:00:43,347
That is exactly the height a blade travels at when the skater in front goes down.

10
00:00:44,048 --> 00:00:48,030
Your lower legs sit in the most exposed position in the sport.

11
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:52,590
You skate in a deep crouch, so your shin is angled forward and leading.

12
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:55,866
You corner with the knee tracking out over the toe.

13
00:00:55,866 --> 00:01:01,261
That makes your kneecap the furthest forward point of your body for most of the lap.

14
00:01:01,261 --> 00:01:08,712
And you do all of this inside a pack, where one fall can bring several skaters down with sharp blades still turning.

15
00:01:08,712 --> 00:01:11,281
That is why leg protection has two jobs.

16
00:01:11,281 --> 00:01:15,970
It has to resist a slicing edge, which is what cut resistant fabric does.

17
00:01:15,970 --> 00:01:21,558
And it has to spread a blunt impact, which is what a hard shell or dense foam pad does.

18
00:01:21,558 --> 00:01:25,475
Kit that does only one of those leaves half the problem open.

19
00:01:26,176 --> 00:01:29,765
Think of your leg protection as one continuous surface.

20
00:01:29,765 --> 00:01:34,726
At the top, knee protection covers the whole kneecap, not just the top half.

21
00:01:34,726 --> 00:01:41,383
Below it, shin protection runs down the front of the shin and overlaps both the sock and the knee pad.

22
00:01:41,383 --> 00:01:43,406
The chain breaks in two places.

23
00:01:43,406 --> 00:01:48,889
Gap one sits between the knee pad and the shin guard, and it opens up when you bend.

24
00:01:48,889 --> 00:01:52,805
Gap two sits between the shin guard and the top of the boot.

25
00:01:52,805 --> 00:01:55,416
That is the one most skaters leave open.

26
00:01:55,416 --> 00:01:59,658
Check both gaps in a deep skating position, not standing upright.

27
00:02:00,358 --> 00:02:06,369
This is the real question behind most searches, and for training the answer is usually yes.

28
00:02:06,369 --> 00:02:13,106
A hard shell football guard covers the front of the shin, survives a long time and is easy to replace.

29
00:02:13,106 --> 00:02:17,399
If that is what you own, use it rather than skating with nothing.

30
00:02:17,399 --> 00:02:18,984
Know the limits, though.

31
00:02:18,984 --> 00:02:24,995
Football guards are cut for a low boot, so the top often finishes well below your knee pad.

32
00:02:24,995 --> 00:02:30,081
A guard held up only by a sock slides sideways when you slide across the ice.

33
00:02:30,081 --> 00:02:33,779
Guards with their own sleeve or strap behave far better.

34
00:02:33,779 --> 00:02:39,394
Most important, the plastic shell does nothing for the calf and the sides of the leg.

35
00:02:39,394 --> 00:02:44,281
So wear a football guard over a cut resistant layer, never instead of one.

36
00:02:44,281 --> 00:02:48,046
And check your competition rules before you race in them.

37
00:02:48,746 --> 00:02:51,294
Protection for the legs comes two ways.

38
00:02:51,294 --> 00:02:57,042
Cut resistant fabric comes as part of a suit, so it is there every time you get dressed.

39
00:02:57,042 --> 00:03:02,399
It covers the whole leg, including the back of the calf, depending on the garment.

40
00:03:02,399 --> 00:03:04,620
But it spreads very little impact.

41
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:10,238
A hard pad spreads impact well, but only across the front of the shin and the kneecap.

42
00:03:10,238 --> 00:03:13,243
Many sports pads are not cut resistant at all.

43
00:03:13,243 --> 00:03:18,011
These are not really alternatives, because they solve different problems.

44
00:03:18,011 --> 00:03:23,041
If you are new and falling often, run both, with proper pads over the fabric.

45
00:03:23,041 --> 00:03:27,418
A bruised kneecap keeps you off the ice longer than a scuffed suit.

46
00:03:27,418 --> 00:03:33,362
As your falls get rarer but faster, slim pads become easier to live with than bulky shells.

47
00:03:34,062 --> 00:03:38,356
A pad only protects the skin it is sitting on when you hit the ice.

48
00:03:38,356 --> 00:03:43,610
And where it sits in the changing room is not where it sits at the end of a slide.

49
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:47,199
So set knee protection with your leg bent, not straight.

50
00:03:47,199 --> 00:03:52,902
Fit it standing upright and it will ride up your thigh the moment you drop into position.

51
00:03:52,902 --> 00:03:54,632
That leaves the joint open.

52
00:03:54,632 --> 00:03:57,836
Bend your knee to roughly the angle you corner at.

53
00:03:57,836 --> 00:03:59,502
Centre the pad on the cap.

54
00:03:59,502 --> 00:04:02,129
Then stand up and watch where it travels.

55
00:04:02,129 --> 00:04:07,063
If it moves more than a little, the strap is too loose or the pad is too big.

56
00:04:07,764 --> 00:04:09,752
Here are two knees side by side.

57
00:04:09,752 --> 00:04:12,672
On the left, the pad is centred on the kneecap.

58
00:04:12,672 --> 00:04:16,152
It was set with the knee bent and then checked standing.

59
00:04:16,152 --> 00:04:19,259
This pad covers the joint that hits the ice first.

60
00:04:19,259 --> 00:04:22,117
On the right, the pad has ridden up the thigh.

61
00:04:22,117 --> 00:04:27,834
The kneecap underneath is left open, and that is what lands first when you go down forwards.

62
00:04:27,834 --> 00:04:32,618
A pad in that position is protecting your thigh, which was never the problem.

63
00:04:33,318 --> 00:04:36,343
For shin protection, work upwards from the boot.

64
00:04:36,343 --> 00:04:39,621
Pull your cut resistant socks up as high as they go.

65
00:04:39,621 --> 00:04:46,617
Look at where the gripper band finishes against your boot cuff, because that varies with the boot and the sock.

66
00:04:46,617 --> 00:04:53,802
If the sock clears the cuff, set the shin guard so its lower edge overlaps the sock rather than starting above it.

67
00:04:53,802 --> 00:04:59,159
If it does not clear the cuff, that bare band is a gap you need to close another way.

68
00:04:59,159 --> 00:05:00,861
Overlap is the whole point.

69
00:05:00,861 --> 00:05:08,046
Two pieces that just touch while you stand will separate by a finger width as soon as you flex your ankle forward.

70
00:05:08,046 --> 00:05:11,324
The same is true where the guard meets the knee pad.

71
00:05:12,024 --> 00:05:14,206
This check takes fifteen seconds.

72
00:05:14,206 --> 00:05:18,108
Get into your lowest skating position in front of a mirror.

73
00:05:18,108 --> 00:05:22,209
Look down the front of both legs and work through four points.

74
00:05:22,209 --> 00:05:23,598
First, the boot line.

75
00:05:23,598 --> 00:05:28,227
Is there bare skin or plain lycra between the boot and the shin guard?

76
00:05:28,227 --> 00:05:29,616
Second, the sock top.

77
00:05:29,616 --> 00:05:34,841
Is the guard overlapping the cut resistant sock, rather than starting above it?

78
00:05:34,841 --> 00:05:36,230
Third, guard to knee.

79
00:05:36,230 --> 00:05:40,727
With your knee bent, does the shin guard still overlap the knee pad?

80
00:05:40,727 --> 00:05:42,050
Fourth, the kneecap.

81
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:46,878
Is the pad centred on the cap in this position, not parked on your thigh?

82
00:05:46,878 --> 00:05:50,515
There is usually a gap the first time anyone does this.

83
00:05:50,515 --> 00:05:52,963
It is almost always at the boot line.

84
00:05:52,963 --> 00:05:55,013
Fix it there, not in a pile up.

85
00:05:55,713 --> 00:06:03,762
LiveLica stocks the cut resistant foundation, such as undersuits and ankle socks, but not hard shin guards or knee pads.

86
00:06:03,762 --> 00:06:07,518
So here is what to look for when you buy pads elsewhere.

87
00:06:07,518 --> 00:06:13,152
Choose a pad that has its own sleeve or a real strap, so it does not rely on a sock.

88
00:06:13,152 --> 00:06:17,512
Choose one slim enough to fit under a race suit without bunching.

89
00:06:17,512 --> 00:06:23,146
Pads go under the suit and over your cut resistant base layer, never on the outside.

90
00:06:23,146 --> 00:06:29,652
Check the shell covers the whole kneecap, because the bottom edge of the cap meets the ice first.

91
00:06:29,652 --> 00:06:33,006
And for juniors, do not size pads up to grow into.

92
00:06:33,006 --> 00:06:38,774
A pad loose enough to last two seasons is loose enough to slide off the cap in a fall.

93
00:06:39,474 --> 00:06:42,506
Most leg contact in short track is a bruise.

94
00:06:42,506 --> 00:06:47,261
A bruise usually settles with rest and a few days off hard cornering.

95
00:06:47,261 --> 00:06:48,777
Some knocks need more.

96
00:06:48,777 --> 00:06:54,220
See a doctor or physiotherapist if you have sharp pain rather than a dull ache.

97
00:06:54,220 --> 00:07:02,007
Go if the knee or shin swells noticeably, or if you feel numbness or pins and needles below the point of contact.

98
00:07:02,007 --> 00:07:08,346
Go if you cannot put weight through the leg, or if the pain is still there after a few days.

99
00:07:08,346 --> 00:07:15,857
And any cut from a blade that is deep, gaping or will not stop bleeding needs medical attention the same day.

100
00:07:15,857 --> 00:07:18,269
Not a plaster and the next session.

101
00:07:18,969 --> 00:07:25,510
Before your next session, put a cut resistant layer on under your suit and treat it as the foundation.

102
00:07:25,510 --> 00:07:31,409
Fit any pad with your knee bent, and make each piece overlap the next rather than just meet.

103
00:07:31,409 --> 00:07:36,604
Then do the mirror check in your lowest position and close whatever gap you find.

104
00:07:36,604 --> 00:07:38,976
It will probably be at the boot line.

105
00:07:38,976 --> 00:07:44,427
Read your own federation's rules for the details, because that part really does vary.

106
00:07:44,427 --> 00:07:50,070
The full guide, with the comparison table and common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

107
00:07:50,070 --> 00:07:52,058
The link is in the description.
